  "text": "We are given the equation:\n\n$$\nz^3 + \\overline{z}^3 = 65\n$$\n\nwhere $ z $ is a complex number with integer real and imaginary parts.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Express $ z $ and $ \\overline{z} $\n\nLet:\n- $ z = x + yi $, where $ x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z} $ (since $ z $ has integer real and imaginary parts),\n- $ \\overline{z} = x - yi $.\n\nNow compute $ z^3 $ and $ \\overline{z}^3 $:\n\n$$\nz^3 = (x + yi)^3 = x^3 + 3x^2(yi) + 3x(yi)^2 + (yi)^3\n= x^3 + 3x^2yi - 3xy^2 - y^3i\n= (x^3 - 3xy^2) + i(3x^2y - y^3)\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\overline{z}^3 = (x - yi)^3 = x^3 - 3x^2yi - 3xy^2 + y^3i\n= (x^3 - 3xy^2) + i(-3x^2y + y^3)\n$$\n\nNow sum the real and imaginary parts:\n\n$$\nz^3 + \\overline{z}^3 = \\left[(x^3 - 3xy^2) + (x^3 - 3xy^2)\\right] + i\\left[(3x^2y - y^3) + (-3x^2y + y^3)\\right]\n$$\n\nSimplify:\n\n- Real part: $ 2(x^3 - 3xy^2) $\n- Imaginary part: $ 2y^3 $\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nz^3 + \\overline{z}^3 = 2(x^3 - 3xy^2) + 2i y^3 = 65\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n2(x^3 - 3xy^2) + 2i y^3 = 65\n$$\n\nThis means the complex number $ z^3 + \\overline{z}^3 = 65 $ implies:\n\n- Real part: $ 2(x^3 - 3xy^2) = 65 $\n- Imaginary part: $ 2y^3 = 0 $\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Solve the equations\n\nFrom the imaginary part:\n\n$$\n2y^3 = 0 \\Rightarrow y = 0\n$$\n\nThen substitute $ y = 0 $ into the real part:\n\n$$\n2(x^3 - 3x \\cdot 0^2) = 2x^3 = 65 \\Rightarrow x^3 = \\frac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBut $ x^3 $ is **not** an integer. Therefore, **no such integer $ x, y $** satisfies the equation.\n\n---\n\n### Final Conclusion\n\nThere is **no** such integer complex number $ z $ that satisfies the given equation.\n\nSo, the **area of the triangle** is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$",
